  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened capacity of the tripartite constituents to design and implement policies to promote gender equality and tackle discrimination, and violence and harassment</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">To boost the workforce participation of women aged 25-34 with family responsibilities, the National Employment Program 2022-2026 aims to increase the employment-to-population ratio for this group by 3 percentage points by 2026. This strategy includes adjusting the legal framework to support work-family balance and expanding childcare services to cover 30% of children under three. A Law on alternative childcare services has been passed, and a National Program for childcare services for children aged 0-3 is under implementation.  
In 2023, Moldova ratified the ILO Violence and Harassment Convention (No. 190). An analysis revealed that, despite a comprehensive legal framework, violence and harassment are not sufficiently incorporated, and inconsistencies exist between national laws and the provisions of the Convention. 
Participating in a joint UN program, the ILO strengthens Moldova&apos;s care sector by providing affordable innovative services and promoting quality employment and sustainable enterprise creation in caregiving and related sectors. Additionally, the ILO supports building a more gender-responsive environment for women entrepreneurs in targeted areas. This includes promoting quality employment in the care economy, advocating for improved working conditions, and supporting women&apos;s participation in the labour market by facilitating access to childcare and other services. Major deliverables include implementing the Programme to Activate Women through three pilots, developing a country profile on care investment needs, diagnosing working conditions in the care sector, formalizing nannies, supporting social protection and alternative childcare services for low-paid women, conducting a legal assessment for the ratification of ILO Convention No. 154, and providing technical assistance to develop an IT system for nanny registration. 
Through these efforts, the ILO contributes to increased social and economic opportunities for women in Moldova.</narrative>
  </description>
